fresh-eyes: independent cold reviewer (subagent, no authorship of this branch), full scope of the one commit covering rename, remove_dir and dir_is_empty against a link.

Verdict LAND.

The reviewer verified the cap-std premise at source rather than
accepting it. A case-insensitive grep for renameat2, renameatx and
RENAME_EXCL across cap-std 4.0.2 and cap-primitives 4.0.2 returns
nothing; the only public rename takes no flags and bottoms out in a
plain rustix::fs::renameat. macOS declares renameatx_np with
RENAME_EXCL, and Linux has renameat2(RENAME_NOREPLACE), so the
operating system can refuse atomically and cap-std cannot ask it to.
rustix, already in the tree, exposes renameat_with, so an atomic
refusal is reachable one layer down. The doc's narrower claim is right;
a broader one would not have been.

The half-guard mutation was isolated per method, so no earlier
assertion could mask a later one. Each of rename, remove_dir and
dir_is_empty is killed by its own assertion, and is_document, remove
and scan by theirs.

The assertions run through the link rather than at it. A guard asking
whether a path is itself a symlink answers no for a real directory
beyond one, and the ambient action then escapes. Asserting on the link
name alone would have left that mutation alive.

Four half-guards still survive, all one shape: a check correct at the
instant it runs, followed by an action that resolves the path a second
time. No deterministic test separates them from correct code. One of
them, re-opening the store root per call, is the regression the module
header records as already fixed once and which no test enforces. Filed
alongside the store-root issue.

197 tests with --all-features, clippy clean, fmt clean.
